AHS-019 — Security Architecture

Document ID: AHS-019
Title: Security Architecture
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

Architectural Views: Logical, Implementation, Deployment

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical Security Architecture of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Security Architecture establishes the principles, responsibilities, and controls required to protect engineering cognition, canonical data, communications, interfaces, decisions, and platform services while maintaining trust, traceability, and governance.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Security principles
Identity
Authentication
Authorization
Confidentiality
Integrity
Availability
Auditability
Trust model
Security governance

This specification does not mandate specific cryptographic algorithms, identity providers, or security products.

3. Canonical Definition

Security is the continuous protection of Atlas participants, canonical objects, cognitive processes, engineering decisions, and communications against unauthorized access, modification, disclosure, disruption, or misuse while preserving engineering trust.

4. Security Objectives

Atlas SHALL ensure:

Confidentiality
Integrity
Availability
Authenticity
Accountability
Traceability
Non-repudiation
Least privilege
5. Security Principles

Every Atlas implementation SHALL follow these principles.

Security by Design

Security SHALL be considered during architecture and design.

Least Privilege

Participants SHALL receive only the permissions required.

Defense in Depth

Multiple independent security controls SHALL be employed.

Zero Trust

No participant SHALL be inherently trusted.

Every interaction SHALL be verified.

Secure by Default

Default configurations SHALL minimize security risk.

Auditability

Security-relevant actions SHALL be recorded.

Separation of Duties

Critical engineering activities SHALL support role separation.

6. Security Domains

Atlas defines six security domains.

6.1 Identity Security

Protects identities of:

Users
Services
Agents
External systems
Organizations

Every participant SHALL possess a unique identity.

6.2 Access Security

Controls:

Authentication
Authorization
Session management
Permission evaluation
6.3 Data Security

Protects:

Canonical Engineering Artifacts
Decision Records
Events
Communications
Knowledge

Controls include:

Confidentiality
Integrity
Classification
Retention
6.4 Communication Security

Protects:

Interfaces
Messages
Events
Streaming data

Communication SHALL maintain:

Integrity
Confidentiality
Authenticity
6.5 Operational Security

Protects:

Runtime services
Infrastructure
Configuration
Monitoring
Deployment
6.6 Governance Security

Protects:

Policies
Audit records
Traceability
Compliance
Decision approvals
7. Canonical Trust Model

Atlas adopts a Zero Trust model.

Every request SHALL verify:

Identity
Authorization
Context
Policy compliance
Security classification

Trust SHALL be continuously evaluated.

8. Identity Model

Every participant SHALL possess:

Element	Description
Identity ID	Globally unique identifier
Identity Type	User, Service, Agent, System
Organization	Owning organization
Roles	Assigned roles
Attributes	Security attributes
Status	Active, Suspended, Revoked
Credentials	Authentication references

Identity SHALL be independent of authentication mechanisms.

9. Authorization Model

Authorization SHALL evaluate:

Identity
Roles
Attributes
Policies
Context
Requested operation
Security classification

Authorization decisions SHALL be auditable.

10. Security Classification

Atlas defines four canonical classifications.

Classification	Description
Public	No restrictions
Internal	Organization-only
Confidential	Restricted engineering information
Restricted	Highly sensitive engineering information

Classification SHALL propagate to derived artifacts unless explicitly reclassified.

11. Audit Architecture

Security-relevant activities SHALL generate audit records.

Examples include:

Authentication
Authorization
Policy changes
Decision approvals
Interface publication
Workflow execution
Administrative actions

Audit records SHALL be immutable.

12. Security Lifecycle
Defined
    │
Implemented
    │
Verified
    │
Operational
    │
Monitored
    │
Updated
    │
Retired

Security controls SHALL be continuously monitored.

13. Incident Management

Security incidents SHALL support:

Detection
Classification
Containment
Investigation
Recovery
Lessons learned

Incident records SHALL be traceable.

14. Security Quality Attributes

Atlas SHALL evaluate:

Confidentiality
Integrity
Availability
Authenticity
Accountability
Non-repudiation
Resilience
Recoverability

Security assessments SHALL be documented.

15. Conformance

An implementation conforms if it:

Implements the canonical trust model.
Maintains unique participant identities.
Enforces authorization.
Protects canonical data.
Preserves immutable audit records.
Documents security governance.
16. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Adaptive trust evaluation.
Continuous authorization.
Hardware-backed identity.
Confidential computing.
Quantum-resistant cryptography.
Cross-organization trust federation.

Extensions SHALL preserve the canonical security principles.